<example xml:id="example-stokes-triangle">
<title>Around a Triangle, Twice</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> for
<me>
\mathbf{F} = (y - z)\,\mathbf{i} + (x + z)\,\mathbf{j}
+ \left(x^2 + y\right)\mathbf{k},
</me>
where <m>C</m> goes from <m>(2,0,0)</m> to <m>(0,2,0)</m> to
<m>(0,0,4)</m> and back to <m>(2,0,0)</m> along straight line segments,
as in <xref ref="fig-stokes-triangle-tikz"/>. Do the computation twice:
directly, and by Stokes' theorem.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,20);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10) -- (15,10);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,19.7) {directly:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {by Stokes' theorem:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the direct computation and one for the computation by Stokes' theorem.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

